    "A": "Develop a persistent-team parallel 3-pass signed radix sorter (for example 11/11/10-bit digits) as a direct alternative to the current fastest 4x8-bit design. Optimize per-thread padded histograms, deterministic prefixing, and disjoint stable scatters for ARM64/4 cores; retain a correct serial/allocation-failure fallback and measure end-to-end against e69f21d, not just sorting throughput.",
    "B": "Investigate end-to-end overhead outside the winning sort path, especially ARM64 sortedness validation and inexpensive generation/loop-level improvements that preserve the exact generated integer sequence and MD5 output. Produce only independently correct, narrowly isolated changes suitable for grafting onto the best radix candidate; do not replace it with the already slower standalone 11/11/10 serial implementation.",
    "C": "Own refinement of e69f21d’s measured-best persistent 4x8-bit OpenMP radix path. Explore cache locality and synchronization costs: histogram/offset layout and padding, allocation reuse/overflow guards, thread-count and small-N dispatch, and safe pass-loop simplification. Keep signed ordering, stability, portable non-OpenMP behavior, and allocation-failure correctness; report each measured variant separately.",
    "G": "Use e69f21d (0.015090 s) as the initial reference. Integrate only a complete measured winner or a small independently validated adjunct from A/B/C; never assume improvements compose and never combine competing full sorters. Re-measure full program time and correctness after every candidate integration, retaining the fastest valid result across all valid input sizes/configurations and exact observable output.",
    "analysis": "Baseline is 0.070154 s. The best admissible result is C’s persistent-team signed 8-bit radix sorter at 0.015090 s; A’s closely related version is 0.015273 s. B’s 11/11/10-bit radix variants are slower at 0.017454–0.017645 s, and blocked serial radix is 0.020273 s. Thus research should start from the 4-pass parallel architecture, while testing the plausible three-pass parallel tradeoff separately and treating validator/overhead work as isolated adjuncts. Correctness must retain exact sorted integers and the benchmark’s resulting MD5 for every valid positive N, without relying on particular measured cases."
